'''Hesperia''' ({{Literal translation|evening|sunset|}}, or broadly, 'the far west'; {{Langx|kp|Kuaráhy-Owá|4=rising sun land}}), known romantically as '''the Hesperides''' ({{Lang-bl|Las Hespérides}}), is a [[Communities of Balisca|community]] within the [[Balisca|Baliscan Confederation]]. Its mainland largely consists of the easternmost portion of the [[Northern Triangle]]. With over thirty-two million inhabitants in March 2026, it is the fifth most populous of the Baliscan communities, having been surpassed by [[Araucaria]] in 2010. Other large cities in the community include [[Syracuse]], [[Cordova]], [[Castello]], and [[Trivero]]. It comprises twelve regions; [[Sicania]], [[Basilisco]], [[Piscaria]], [[Piedmont]], [[Campostella]], [[Acacias]], [[Aprica]], [[Las Peñas]], [[Tartessia]], [[Trás-as-Montanyas]], [[Guarapiranga]] and [[Las Serras]].  

Hesperia's topography is dominated by the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] mountain chain, which takes up roughly 75% of Hesperia's total territory and rapidly descend into the narrow lowlands along [[Levante, Balisca|the coast]]. This narrow strip of land between the Aethiopic Ocean and the Matarrañya / Cañadapirén, called the [[Baliscan Piemont]], dominates in terms of population and economic resources and is the cultural and political centers from which Illypnian settlement expanded in the 19th century following the success of the [[Baliscan War of Independence|''Resistencía'']]. The coast, called the [[Levante, Balisca|Levante]] ({{Literal translation|the east}}) is a labyrinth of [[W:Ria|rias]], inlets, twisting peninsulas, and islands.

Originally, what is now Hesperia was sparsely populated by several indigenous tribes. Illypnian exploration began with its discovery by explorer [[Carlu Arceru]] on 28 June 1502, on behalf of the [[Kingdom of Volisania]] (part of the then [[United Kingdoms of Volisania]]). During this time, there were several parties simultaneous exploring the [[Northern Triangle]], including the Mesogean [[W:Merchant republics|merchant republics]] (including [[Tartessian Republic|Tartessia]]), and displaced [[W:Mudéjar|Mudéjar]], [[W:Moriscos|Moriscos]], [[W:Sepheradic Jews|Sephardic Jews]], and [[Catharism|Cathars]], all under the patronage of Volisania. Following several failed settlement attempts, the Volisanians established [[Sant Àgata]] ('[[W:Agatha of Sicily|Saint Agatha]]') as the first permanent Illypnian settlement in Abyala. In the face of the [[Great Disappointment]], the Mesogean trade settlements banded together as the [[Hesperian League]]. The outbreak of the [[Volisanian Civil War]] (which saw conflict in Balisca), the League played a pivotal role in the development of the region during the [[Volisanya Boba|''Volisanya Boba'']] period. Hesperia would later lead the resistance against Volisan attempts at colonial reconquest, becoming a founding member of the [[Northern Confederation]] in 1808. 

== Etymology ==
[[File:Mengs,_Hesperus_als_Personifikation_des_Abends.jpg|left|thumb|196x196px|''Hesperus as Personification of the Evening Star'' by [[Anton Raphael Mengs]] (1765).]]
The name means ''originating from Hesperos'' (evening). ''Hesperos'', or ''Vesper'' in [[Mesogean language|Mesogean]], is the origin of the name [[W:Hesperus|Hesperus]], the evening star (i.e. the planet [[W:Venus|Venus]]) as well as having a shared root with the Mercian word "west".

==History==
===Pre-Mesogean arrival===
===Mesogean exploration===
{{Main|Colonization of Balisca}}
The Mesogean presence in what is now Hesperia began with the landing of [[Carlu Arceru]] on 28 June 1502, a [[Thermessa|Thermessan]] navigator commissioned by the [[Kingdom of Volisania]], which was at the time a part of the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania|United Kingdoms]]. Arceru made landfall on [[Cap Salvador]], in what is now known as [[Sicania]], claiming the territory in the name of the Volisanian Crown. Arceru's voyage was part of a broader surge of Mesogean exploration in the [[Aethiopian Ocean#Northern Aethiopic|Northern Aethopic]], spurred by competition with Bizkarran expeditions to the south and Burawi ventures along the Kaftian coast.
[[File:Desembarque de Cabral.jpg|left|thumb|[[Carlu Arceru]] landing in [[Cabo Salvatore]] in 1502, ushering in the beginning of Illypnian colonization.]]
Arceru described the island as "''tierra alta de brumas y silencio''" (high land of mist and silence), and he recorded the presence of several loosely organized Indigenous coastal communities, most notably the [[Tayahuí]] and [[Naburá]], whom he described as adept canoe-builders and deep-sea fishermen. His detailed logbooks, which survived in the [[Archivio Real de Hesperides|''Archivio Real de Hesperides'']], became essential guides for future expeditions and helped secure Volisania's claim to the [[Levante, Balisca|Hesperian coast]] ahead of rival Meso-Illypnian colonial powers.

Over the following two decades, the coastlines of Hesperia were surveyed by a variety of Mesogean powers operating under [[Abyala Charter|charters and commercial ambitions]]. These included expeditions funded by the [[Levantine republics|Levantine maritime republics]] of the eastern Mesogean (the [[Malerno|Republic of Malerno]], the [[Tartessian Republic]], and independent merchant houses from [[W:Sfax|Sfax]], [[W:Salerno|Salerno]], and [[W:Valencia|Valencia]]. Among these was the famed Morisco navigator [[Benjamín al-Haurí]], who led a 1511 voyage that mapped the [[Gulf of tba]] and engaged in some of the earliest documented barter exchanges with the inland [[Itziqa]] peoples. Though technically subjects of the Volisanian Crown, many of these expeditions operated in a legal grey area, benefitting from the Volisanian granting of charters to [[W:Chartered companies|companies]] for exploratory and mercantile ventures.

Significantly, these early expeditions also served as escape routes for religious minorities and displaced communities fleeing persecution and religious conflict in [[Kafto-Illypnia]]. Following the [[TBA (war of religion)]] (15xx) and subsequent forced conversions, waves of [[W:Mudéjars|Mudéjars]], [[W:Sephardic Jews|Sephardic Jews]], [[W:Moriscos|Moriscos]], and [[Catharism|Cathars]]—many of whom spoke [[Mozbayani language|Mozbayani]] or various [[W:Judeo-Catalan|Judeo-Volisanian]] dialects—joined or funded exploratory missions in hopes of founding new lives abroad. The Kingdom of Volisania, eager to populate and stabilize its peripheral holdings, tacitly allowed these exiles to settle in Abyala.

Despite several failed colonization attempts due to disease, hostile terrain, and Indigenous resistance, a permanent foothold was finally secured in 1534 with the founding of [[Sant'Àjita]] (named after [[W:Agatha of Sicily|Saint Agatha, patroness of Sicily]]). Established on a natural harbor along the [[Bahía de Anzuelos]], Sant'Àjita became the first enduring Illypnian settlement on the continent, and the future capital of the Hesperian League. The foundation of Sant'Àjita marked the beginning of organized Mesogean presence in Hesperia and catalyzed the development of a regional maritime economy that would later give rise to the Hesperian League, and established a precedent for multiethnic settlement in Abyala. The settlement was led by Captain [[Martinu de Sclàfani]], a former Volisanian corsair in the [[Levant]] turned colonial administrator, and included a diverse population of Thermessan craftsmen, persecuted Muslims and Christian [[W:New Christians|heretics]], and Sephardic financiers.

==== Thalassocratic confederation ====
{{Main|Hesperian League}}

The [[Hesperian League]] emerged from the strategic realignment that followed the [[Great Disappointment]] of 1564. The discovery that the western coast of the New World was already subject to colonization by Fosian powers—principally the [[Kingdom of Zong]], the [[Jyunhap]], and the [[Three Luoyan States]]—produced a crisis among the Mesogean colonial powers. The assumption that the [[Lurando-Abyala|New World]] was an exclusively Kafto-Illypnian sphere of expansion was overturned; the continent was revealed to be a contested space, with civilisations of comparable organisational capacity approaching from the opposite direction.

The Illypnian response was to accelerate the colonisation of territories already claimed and to consolidate colonial holdings against potential Fosian encroachment. In Abyala, this imperative produced the Hesperian League—a thalassocratic defensive and commercial confederation between the trading posts ("[[W:Factory (trading post)|factories]]") of the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania]], the [[Empire of Burawa]], and the merchant companies operating on charters and concessions from the Volisan and Burawi Crowns. The [[Almogavars Company]] was the most prominent of these chartered entities, though numerous smaller merchant houses participated in the League's arrangements. The League was initially an alliance of convenience—a loose association of traders and towns that advanced mutual commercial interests. Its members faced common challenges: protection against raids from indigenous peoples, the exploration of the [[Marañón basin]], and the need to establish a defensive perimeter against potential Fosian expansion across the isthmus. The League's founding arrangements allowed the participating colonies and companies to enjoy duty-free trade among themselves, mutual protection, and diplomatic privileges in their communities and trade routes.

Over the following decades, the League gradually developed a common legal system governing its merchants and goods. The [[Consulat de Mar de Hesperia]], established in 1592, provided a maritime court that adjudicated disputes between League members regardless of their metropolitan origin. The League also operated its own armies for mutual defense and aid—the [[Hesperian Condotta]], a force of contracted soldiers drawn from the diverse populations of the League's ports, answered to the League's council rather than to any metropolitan authority.

Reduced barriers to trade resulted in mutual prosperity, which fostered economic interdependence, kinship ties between merchant families, and deeper political integration. By the end of the 17th century, these factors had solidified the League into a cohesive political organization—one that remained formally subject to Volisanian and Burawi sovereignty but operated with substantial autonomy in practice. The League's ports—[[Sant'Àjita]], [[Alessandria]], [[Portulegnu]], and [[Portu Mezquita]] among them—formed a network of commercial and defensive interests that transcended their metropolitan origins.

=== Illypno-Fosian colonial conflicts and Metropolitan decline ===
{{Main|First War of the Isthmus}}

By the early 18th century, the strategic position of the Hesperian League's metropolitan patrons had deteriorated significantly. Both Volisania and Burawa faced crises that would undermine their capacity to govern their colonial possessions and set the stage for the League's gradual drift toward autonomy. The [[First War of the Isthmus]] (1711–1716) was the first major conflict between Illypnian and Fosian colonial powers in the [[Lurando-Abyala|New World]]. The war centered on control of [[Sant Cristòfor]], the largest port on the [[Isthmus of Balboa]]—the narrow land bridge connecting [[Abyala]] and [[Lurandia]]. The [[Kingdom of Zong]], the dominant maritime power of eastern Fosia, had established the colony of [[Tamsui]] on the western coast of the Lurandia. Zong's commercial interests required a reliable route to Illypnian markets; the long overland voyage around the southern tip of Lurandia was costly, dangerous, and time-consuming. The Isthmus of Balboa offered a shortcut: control of the isthmus would allow Zong to transport goods overland between the western (Fosian-facing) and eastern (Illypnian-facing) coasts.

In 1711, Zong launched an expedition to capture Sant Cristòfor. The attack represented a direct challenge to Illypnian colonial dominance in the region. Volisania, the preeminent Illypnian naval power, responded with a full mobilisation of its forces. Burawa, bound to Volisania by the strategic alignment that had developed since the 1680s, committed fully to the war, dispatching naval forces, ground troops, and financial resources to support the Illypnian coalition. [[Lacashe]], the Burawi protectorate in Abyala, likewise contributed forces as a Burawi ally.

The war quickly expanded beyond a local colonial skirmish. [[Breisland]] and [[Merica]], two Illypnian powers that had historically competed with Volisania for colonial advantage, chose to support Zong rather than their fellow Illypnians. The calculation was straightforward: a Volisanian defeat would weaken the kingdom that dominated Illypnian colonial politics, even if the immediate beneficiary was a Fosian power. Zong forces succeeded in blockading Sant Cristòfor in 1713 and eventually captured the port after a sustained siege. The Illypnian coalition mounted a counter-offensive that surrounded the Zong expeditionary force, and Zong opened negotiations rather than face destruction.

The [[Treaty of Remoria]] (1716) ended the war. The terms represented a compromise, but one that reflected poorly on Volisania. Zong agreed to withdraw from San Cristóbal, but in exchange received docking and passage rights through Volisanian ports—access to the Aethiopean Ocean and Illypnian trade networks that Zong had sought all along. For Volisania, the outcome was humiliating. The kingdom had mobilised its vast naval resources, committed to a major war, and emerged having granted its opponent the very concessions that war had been fought to prevent. The perception that Volisania had been outmanoeuvred at the negotiating table contributed to domestic unrest that would culminate in the [[Volisanian Civil War]] of 1736.

For Burawa, the war had been costly in blood and treasure but produced no tangible gains. The kingdom had honoured its alliance with Volisania and demonstrated its commitment to the Illypnian coalition, but the alliance itself seemed increasingly questionable. The treaty's terms suggested that Volisania could not reliably protect Illypnian interests. Burawa's weakness, however, predated the war—and was considerably more severe.

The [[Imperial Split]] of 1709 had divided the Burawi kingdom into two states: the eastern [[Kingdom of Burawa]] under [[Iacob V]] and the western [[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa]] under the child king [[Cali IV]] with his mother [[Sahra]] as regent. To secure the support of the merchant houses, the Hoga aristocracy, and the Church during the succession crisis, Iacob V had been compelled to create a [[four-house parliament]]—a representative body that shared power with the crown. The four houses were the [[House of the King]], representing royal authority; the [[House of the Church]], representing ecclesiastical interests; the [[House of the Hogas]], representing the landed aristocracy; and the [[House of the Landowners]], representing the merchant and planter elite.

This arrangement was unprecedented in Burawi history. For centuries, the kingdom had been governed as an absolutist monarchy; the parliament represented a fundamental break with tradition. The question of how the new system would function, and whether it could function at all, defined Burawi politics for the following decades. The kingdom's attention turned inward; the capacity to govern distant colonial possessions diminished accordingly.

==== Volisanian civil war and consolidation ====
{{Main|Volisanya Boba|Maritime Republic of Hesperia}}

The outbreak of the [[Volisanian Civil War]] in 1736 fundamentally altered the relationship between the Hesperian League and its metropolitan patrons. As the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania]] fractured between the [[Traditionalist]] faction—loyal to the deposed [[House of Domènech]]—and the [[Reformist]] coalition backing the upstart [[House of Montcada]], the Crown's capacity to govern its colonial possessions collapsed. The League, which had operated under Volisanian charter for nearly two centuries, found itself cut off from both legitimate authority and material support. The Empire of Burawa, the League's other metropolitan patron, faced its own internal pressures during this period. The long-running [[Four House Era|Burawi political crisis]] and the subsequent [[Restoration Revolution]] (1743–1745) further diverted the already minimal metropolitan attention and resources away from the Abyalan colonies. The League was effectively orphaned—nominally subject to two metropolitan powers, neither of which could exercise effective authority over it.[[File:Don Ismael el-Nabulsi.jpg|left|thumb|360x360px|[[Don Ismael el-Nabulsi]], a [[Maroon pirates|Maroon corsair]] leader during the [[First War of the Isthmus]] ({{Circa|1719}})]]This period—the [[Volisanya Boba]] or "Silly Volisania"—lasted from 1736 until the final resolution of the civil war in 1801. During these six decades, the Hesperian League developed the institutions and practices of an independent maritime republic. The League's governance structures, which had evolved to coordinate commercial and defensive interests among diverse members, now served as the framework for de facto self-government. The [[Hesperian Parliament]], a rotating council of delegates from the member settlements, became the League's sovereign authority in matters of trade, diplomacy, and defense. The League's merchant fleet expanded dramatically as Volisanian shipping was disrupted by the civil war. Hesperian vessels carried goods between [[Illypnia]], [[Kaftia]], and [[Abyala]], often under neutral flags that allowed them to trade with belligerents on all sides. The League's ports became hubs of international commerce, drawing merchants, refugees, and fortune-seekers from across the known world. The [[Arsenal of Alessandria]], expanded during this period, became one of the largest shipbuilding and armament complexes outside of Illypnia.

The period also saw the flourishing of the Hesperian corsairs—semi-state-sanctioned maritime raiders who targeted shipping belonging to Mesogean factions and their allies. These crews, drawn from the diverse populations of the League's ports, included Sephardic Jews, Moriscos, [[Mozbayana|Mozbayans]], and [[Maroons]] from the [[Maroon Confederacy]]. [[Don Ismael el-Nabulsi]], a commander of mixed Sephardic and [[Imfezzan peoples|Imfezzan]] ancestry, became the most famous of these corsairs, his raids on Reformist shipping earning him legendary status in Hesperian folklore.[[File:Francesco Guardi - The Entrance to the Arsenal in Venice - Google Art Project.jpg|thumb|The [[Alessandria Arsenal|Arsenal of Alessandria]], a complex of shipyards and armories. The Arsenal was responsible for the bulk of the Maritime Republic's naval power, and was one of the earliest large-scale industrial enterprises in pre-Confederation history. Today it is part of [[Naval Station Arayamar]].]]
In response to increasing threats from privateers, and the proliferation of piracy, the Hesperian provinces adopted the [[Defensive Charter of 1764]], forming for the first time a centralized naval fleet and a unified fortification system. This resulted in the construction of advanced bastions and [[W:Dry dock|dry dock]] facilities across cities like [[Cordova]], Alessandria, [[Portulegnu]] and [[Maracaju]], creating one of the most sophisticated naval defense systems outside of the Illypno-Fosian metropoles. The Republic experienced continued economic growth throughout mid-to-late 18th century, largely due to its strategic geographical position. Hesperia pioneered some of the earliest experiments in [[W:Proto-industrialization|proto-industrialization]] in [[Abyala]], allowing it to grow into a prominent hub for re-export trade''',''' [[W:Shipbuilding|shipbuilding]]''',''' and artisanal manufacturing.  

Hesperian cities were instrumental in the production of diverse goods, including textiles, tools, preserved fish, and maritime equipment, which were then exported to emerging regional and international markets. This economic prosperity coincided with small-scale migration from Northern Illypnia, [[Southern Kaftia]], and the [[Levant|Mesogean Levant]]. This influx of people significantly diversified Hesperia's cultural and religious landscape. While Petran Catholicism remained the faith of a majority of the population during this time, the Republic generally maintained relatively liberal policies of [[W:Religious tolerance|religious tolerance]]. This was largely a pragmatic response to the diverse merchant class and their powerful families that had emerged during the consolidation of the [[Hesperian League|League]]. 

The League's relationship with the [[Maroon Confederacy]] deepened during this period. The Maroons, who had established their own autonomous communities in the Baliscan interior, shared with the League an interest in resisting metropolitan authority and maintaining the freedom of the seas. Commercial ties, anti-slavery cooperation, and mutual defense agreements bound the two entities together, creating the foundation for their later alliance in the independence struggle. The emergent [[Hesperian Navy]] coordinated closely with Hesperian corsairs in the [[Battle of Ivorra]], where a Volisanian convoy of six ships was intercepted and destroyed ''en route'' to Cerdanya. The League would leverage this emerging naval dominance to maintain open lines between the itself and its allies across the Aethiopic and within the New World. These events culminated in the [[Battle of Punta Rojiza]] (February 1720), where Spanish infantry attempting to land at (present-day [[Piemont]]) were decisively defeated by a coalition of Hesperian irregulars and corsairs led by [[Zahra de Cádiz]], a former Maroon captive turned naval tactician and [[W:Folk hero|folk hero]]. Her victory became emblematic of the unified and multiethnic resistance that characterized the Hesperian defiance of Spanish imperialism.

=== ''Reconquesta'' and early confederation ===
{{Main|Reconquesta|Northern Confederation}}

The resolution of the Volisanian Civil War in 1801, with the Reformist victory and the establishment of the federal [[Community of Volisania]] via the [[Law of the Community of Volisania and Status of the Crown Acts 1801 and 1812]], raised the question of the Baliscan colonies' status. The new Volisanian state, exhausted by sixty-five years of conflict, lacked the resources to immediately reassert control over territories that had governed themselves for generations. However, the reactionary ''[[Restaurasionistas]]'', a signficant parliamentary force in Volisanian politics, were adamant in their intentions to eventually restore metropolitan authority.

In 1808, the newly centralized [[Community of Volisania]] launched the ''[[Reconquesta]]'', dispatching expeditionary forces in an attempt to reassert control over it's effectively independent colonies in [[Lurando-Abyala]]. The campaign began with landings in Bayara and an attempted naval blockade of Hesperian ports. The League, which had operated as an independent power for over seven decades, refused to submit.

The [[Siege of Sant'Àjita]] (1808–1809) became the defining moment of Hesperian resistance. The city's fortifications, expanded over decades of autonomy, held against naval bombardment. The League's corsairs—now operating as a formal navy—harried Volisanian supply lines and prevented reinforcement. The [[Maroon Confederacy]] entered the war on the League's side, providing crucial support and drawing on decades of cooperation.

The turning point came with the [[Battle of Punta Rojiza]] (February 1809), where Volisanian forces attempting to land at [[Piemont]] were decisively defeated by a coalition of Hesperian irregulars and Maroon auxiliaries. The battle was led by [[Zahra de Cádiz]], a former Maroon captive turned naval commander whose victory became emblematic of the multiethnic resistance to Volisanian reconquest.

The failure of the reconquest forced Volisania to the negotiating table. The [[Treaty of Daramazán]] (1809) formally recognized the independence of the Baliscan territories, establishing the [[Northern Confederation]] as a sovereign state. Hesperia was one of the founding members, alongside the [[Cerdanyan Commonwealth]], the [[United Provinces of Bayara|United Provinces]], and the [[Maroon Confederacy]].

===Post-confederation===
====Confederal Question and civil War====
{{Main|Confederal Question}}

{{See also|Pastry War|Baliscan Civil War}}Following the conclusion of the Baliscan War of Independence, Hesperia emerged as one of the principal architects of the Northern Confederation, embracing its role as a [[W:Maritime republic|maritime republic]] and a bulwark of confederal unity. While initially aligned with the decentralized principles outlined in the [[Articles of Confederation]], Hesperia’s commercial elite, reformist intelligentsia, and naval leadership grew increasingly concerned regarding the limitations of the confederal system—particularly its inability to mediate regional disputes, enforce political reforms, and protect important confederal maritime trade routes. 
[[File:Battle of Miramar.png|thumb|The [[Battle of Miramar|1828 bombardment of Miramar]] ([[Aprica]]) was first engagement of the [[Baliscan Civil War]]]]
Hesperia subsequently became the most vocal proponent of confederal constitutional reform, viewing a strengthened central government as necessary, not only to preserve the republic's economic interests, but also to preserve the internal stability of the entire confederation. This stance was intensified by growing tensions in the Estuarian wetlands, where Hesperian merchants and settlers—many of whom were Catalan-speaking Cathars—faced harassment from Aragonese-speaking Catholic loyalists. In 1823, the murder of a Catalan pastry chef in [[Xàbia]] by a Catholic mob catalyzed a series of reprisals that spiraled into [[Pastry War|open ethnic conflict]]. Under pressure, the [[Confederal Congress]] dispatched a neutral force-the [[Cerdanyan Legion]]-in an effort to stabilize the region. Confederal intervention imposed the [[Treaty of Navassa]], which established a system of military governance across the Estuarian territories. However, the treaty’s ambiguous terms and lack of strong confederal protection contributed to continued unrest. The Cerdanyan Legion's eventual withdrawal from the wetlands due to funding disputes left Hesperian officials increasingly frustrated with the Confederation's administrative weakness.

In response to increasing unrest, Hesperian delegates convened the [[1826 Confederal Convention]], which produced the [[Compromise of 1827]]. This agreement proposed a significant overhaul of the Articles of Confederation, replacing them with a federated charter that would grant greater powers to a centralized government, including fiscal authority, judicial oversight, and an integrated military structure. Delegates representing Hesperia and Cerdanya supported the compromise almost unanimously, while the Bayaran provinces (with the exception of the [[Carpinchera Republic|Province of Macaio]]) boycotted the convention in favor of secession from the confederation. When the [[Xunta Nacionalista]] formally denounced the Articles on 6 July 1828, the Confederal Congress ordered a mobilization for war. Hesperia’s maritime dominance ensured control over key ports and supply lines, while its natural alpine defenses would prove difficult for the attempted Bayaran incursions through the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]]. One major exception was the [[Cerroitá Pass]], through which Bayaran forces launched an ambitious but ultimately unsuccessful campaign to seize the [[Alpujarra]] and split the confederation, culminating in the [[Siege of Castejón]]. The siege was broken with the decisive intervention of the [[Maroon Confederacy]], which had longstanding ties to Hesperia's powerful naval forces and anti-slavery activity.

Throughout the war, Hesperian forces adopted a strategy of [[W:Total war|total war]] and [[W:Attrition warfare|attrition]] against the Xunta. While effective, this strategy resulted in widespread devastation across Bayara, particularly in the [[Western Plains (Balisca)|rural savannas]] of Western Bayara. Hesperian troops destroyed key transport infrastructure, saltworks, and plantation estates to permanently dismantle Bayara’s oligarchic power base. By the war's end, Hesperia had seized territorial control of [[Trás-as-Montanyas]], a territory across the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]], and it was annexed under the [[Treaty of Bixessarri]] in 1830.

The treaty formally ended the war and the put to rest the "Confederal Question", establishing the framework for a new [[Anyos Federales|federal constitutional order]]. Hesperia emerged from the conflict not only as a military victor but as a dominant force in the shaping of the postwar Confederation. The Republic’s centralized naval and commercial institutions became models for postwar integration, and Hesperia assumed a leading role in the reconstruction of Bayara and the protection of Estuarian autonomy.

