In 2019, Hesperia alone was responsible for 30% of Balisca’s total shipping activity. As a mainstay to the economy, the total value of international trade conducted in Hesperia was estimated to be well over 55% of the country’s GDP, making it one of the most trade dependent economies in the world. The [[Port of Alessandria]] is the busiest in Balisca, handling ? million tons of cargo in 2019. 

In the first quarter of 2025, Hesperia had a GDP per capita ([[W:Purchasing power parity|PPP]]) of $43,001, the third-highest in the confederation. At the same time, Hesperia overall had an average poverty rate of 2.9%, one of the lowest rates in Balisca, and Latin America as a whole. While progress has been made, substantial income disparities between urban population centers and [[Rural society in Balisca|rural communities]] remain, and worsened during the {{W|Great Recession}}. Adversely, Hesperia has the highest levels of income inequality in Balisca, and in 2024 earned a {{W|GINI}} score of 26.2 (compared to the Baliscan average of 18.9).

Hesperia's development has been marked by the growth of the {{W|services sector}} since the 1980s, and in particular by the growth of innovative activities in the sector of services to enterprises and in {{W|credit}} and {{W|financial services}}. At the same time, the strong industrial vocation of the region has declined to some degree, with the rise of the [[W:Exotechnology|exotechnology]] and robotics industry in [[Cerdanya]]. The community is well known for its innovative cooperatives, and for its influential and high-quality automobile, machinery, food, design and {{W|fashion industry}}. Hesperia is the world's x-largest manufacturing country, with a significant concentration of global multinational corporations and a large number of dynamic {{W|small and medium-sized enterprises}}, notoriously clustered in several {{W|industrial districts}}, which are spread throughout the community. 

===Energy, infrastructure, and environment===
{{Main|Energy in Balisca|Transport in Balisca|Environment of Balisca|AVB}}

[[File:Hesperia wind farm.png|left|thumb|245x245px|The [[Hesperia Eni 6]] wind farm off the coast of [[Piedmont]]]]
Electricity in Hesperia is produced mainly via {{W|thorium-based nuclear power}}, which accounts for the source of around half of the total amount of electric energy produced in the community. Another important source is {{W|hydroelectric power}}, which was practically the only source of electricity until the 1960s and the proliferation of nuclear energy technology. 

In the last decade, Hesperia has made significant strides in {{W|renewable energy commercialization}}, and has become one of the world's largest producers of {{W|renewable energy}}, ranking as the second largest producer in the Baliscan Confederation and the tba in the world. Wind power and geothermal power are also important sources of electricity in the country. Renewable sources account for the 48.6% of all electricity produced in Hesperia, with wind alone reaching 22.6%, followed by hydro at 15.7%, solar at 4.1%, [[W:Bioenergy|bioenergy]] at 3.5%, and [[W:Geothermal energy|geothermal]] at 1.6%. The rest of the national demand is covered by developing renewable energy sources, such as {{W|wave power}}.
[[File:Atlantic Axis hsr.png|thumb|[[Alcántara|Alcántara ''Tramontana'']] high speed locomotive, with a maximum speed of 400 km/h (249 mph), in [[Piedmont]]]]
In 2021, the transport sector in Hesperia generated a turnover of about 119.4 billion sol, employing 935,700 persons in 83,700 enterprises. Regarding the community's road network, in 2022 there were {{convert|368721|km}} of serviceable roads in Hesperia, including {{convert|2317|km}} of {{W|motorways}}, publicly-owned and operated through ''[[Atlantia]]''. In 2021, about 1,919,554 passenger cars (90 cars per 1,000 people; the least number of personal vehicles per capita in Balisca) and 782,732 goods vehicles circulated on the community's road network.

Hesperia pioneered the construction of the [[Autopista 1|first Baliscan motorway]], establishing the so-called [[Autopista of Balisca|''Autopista'']], reserved for fast traffic and for motor vehicles only. The crossings of the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] are important routes for Baliscan transportation, as the mountains form a significant natural barrier between the community and neighboring [[Bayara]] and [[Estuaria]]. Railway routes crossing the range began operations in 1916 with the [[Trans-Atlántido Railroad]] with its central [[Basilicata Rail Tunnel]], followed in 1923 by the [[tba Tunnel]] and the [[tba Tunnel]] in 1937. As part of the [[New Railway Link (Balisca)|New Railway Link]] through the Matarrañya / Cañadapirén (NCF) in 2009 the [[tba Base Tunnel]] opened, and the [[Corcovado Tunnel]] opened on 2 November 2022. The world's longest undersea tunnel, [[Joajuaba Tunnel|''Joajuaba'']], which crosses the shallow [[Salsipuedes Sea]] to link the islands of [[Pindorama]] and [[Sicania]], opened on 10 May 2022. 

The community's inland waterways network has a length of {{convert|800|km}} for commercial traffic in 2022.

The community's railway network, which is cooperatively-owned and operated, in 2021 totaled {{convert|16,529|km}} of which {{convert|11,727|km}} is [[W:Rail electrification|electrified]], and on which 4,802 locomotives and railcars traverse. The operator of [[AVB|high-speed trains]] in Hesperia is ''[[Transmatarrañya]]'', a cooperative within the [[Baliscan Railways Group|Baliscan Railways]] [[W:Cooperative federation|cooperative federation]]. Higher-speed trains are divided into three categories: ''Frizza russa'' ("red arrow") trains operate at a maximum speed of 300 km/h on dedicated high-speed tracks; ''Frizza argenta'' ("silver arrow") trains operate at a maximum speed of 250 km/h on both high-speed and mainline tracks; and ''Frizza janca'' ("white arrow") trains on high-speed regional lines at a maximum speed of 200 km/h.

