In the [[2025 Baliscan census]], Hesperia was determined to have a population of 23,572,328. Its rate of population growth has been the lowest in the Baliscan Confederation for the last decade, averaging 0.4% growth per year. High fertility and birth rates persisted until the 1970s, after which they started to decline. The population rapidly aged; by 2010, one in six Hesperians were over 65 years old, and the country currently has the twentieth oldest population in the world, with a median age of 42.1 years. However, in recent years Hesperia has experienced significant growth in birth rates, tied to increased immigrant populations. The {{W|total fertility rate}} has also climbed from an all-time low of 1.18 children per woman in 1995 to 1.58 in 2024, albeit still below the {{W|replacement rate}} of 2.1 and considerably below the high of 5.06 children born per woman in 1883. Nevertheless, the total fertility rate is expected to reach 1.6–1.8 by 2026. 

Since the early 2000s, Hesperia has been experiencing what is now known as the "Great Stagnation", the increasing emigration of residents out of Hesperia to other areas of Balisca, and the decline of immigration numbers from overseas. Common reasons for residents leaving Hesperia include the high [[Balisca Consumer Price Index|cost of living]], [[Baliscan housing shortage|scarcity of housing]], as well as increasing extreme weather events (such as [[W:South Atlantic tropical cyclones|North Aethiopic tropical cyclones]]).

It is estimated that Hesperia's population will peak at 28 million people in 2040, upon which become the second-least populous country in the Confederation, behind [[Araucaria]] and ahead of [[Estuaria]]. About 95 percent of the community's population lives in urban areas, with 48 percent living in [[Augusta Corridor|Metropolitan Alessandria]] alone. 

Also current to 2025, 78.7% of the people of Hesperia claimed [[Baliscans|Baliscan nationality]], and 85% declared that they speak [[Baliscano]] at home. 40.24% of the almost 24 million inhabitants of Hesperia declared to be descendants of another single ancestry than Italian. That number includes 1.3 million who declared to be [[Breislandic Baliscans|Breislanders]] as a national-ethnic identification (489,000 as single ethnicity and 1,200,000 a second ethnicity) and 700,000 Sugihans (575,000 as single ethnicity). Recognized minorities numbered around 9% of the population, including around 3 million Basques (2.4 million as a single ethnicity), while permanent {{W|foreign resident}}s (those who have yet to be naturalized) number 2.4 million (around 5% out of Hesperia's total population). Among them, the largest group are [[Imfezzan Baliscans|Imfezzans]], closely followed by [[Asikyira|Asikyirans]] and [[Ayori Baliscans|Ayori]]. 

Hesperia's median life expectancy at birth is 84.1 years (85.3 for females, and 83.8 for males), which is the highest in Balisca. 
===Immigration, ancestry and ethnicity===
{{Main|Ethnography of Balisca|Immigration to Balisca}}

[[File:Italian_immigrants_Hesperia.png|left|thumb|263x263px|An {{W|Italian}} immigrant from {{W|Sicily}} at the immigration station at [[Lipari Island]], [[Alessandria]] (1908)]]
Hesperia has long been both literally and figuratively the primary gateway into the Baliscan Confederation. It was the principle point of entrance for all immigrants entering the Baliscan Confederation from across the Aethiopic Ocean from 1855 to 1878 (after which it was overtaken by [[Castejón]]), and served as the principal terminus of [[W:Transatlantic migration|transatlantic routes]] sailed by [[w:Ocean liner|ocean liners]] in the North Aethiopic. [[Lipari Island]] was an [[W:Immigration center|immigration entry center]] located in the port of Alessandria that processed and issued documents immigrants awaiting entry into the Baliscan Confederation. Between 1890 and 1930, 4.1 million people passed through its halls, and almost 2.8 million of these immigrants would go on to settle in Hesperia.[[File:Lipari island immigration center.png|thumb|The [[Hotel per Immigrati]] immigration processing center on [[Lipari Island]], [[Alessandria]] (1912)]]The number and composition of the population was relatively stable until 1849, when the confederal government, after adopting [[W:Free migration|free migration]] policies, embarked on a campaign to attract foreign immigration to populate the country. This confederal policy remains in place. At first the number of immigrants was modest compared to other countries such as the [[United States]] (though the number of immigrants was steadily increasing as they moved to the rural areas to settle and to found colonies like those of [[Italian Baliscans|Italian]], [[German Baliscans|German]], [[Swiss Baliscans|Swiss]], or [[French Baliscans|French]] origin), but in the 1870s, due to the economic crisis in Europe, it started to increase, peaking in the years between 1890 and 1930. Unofficial records show that, during the 1860s, 360,000 immigrants arrived in Hesperia, while in the 1880s the net number increased to 1.4 million, almost doubling the population of the country in that decade.

Between 1857 and 1950, over 10 million immigrants arrived in Balisca, making it the country with the second largest immigration wave in the world (only second to the United States), and ahead of such other areas of new settlement such as [[Argentina]], [[W:Brazil|Brazil]], [[W:Australia|Australia]], [[W:New Zealand|New Zealand]], [[W:Venezuela|Venezuela]] and [[W:Colombia|Colombia]] and permanently changing the ethnography of Hesperia. Waves of immigrants from European countries continued to arrive unabated through late 19th and early 20th centuries. Over 30 percent of the country's population was born overseas by 1920, and half of the population in Alessandria and Cordova was foreign-born.

