=== Religion ===
{{Main|Religion in Balisca}}

{{Pie chart|thumb=right|caption=Religion in Hesperia ([[2025 Baliscan census|2025 census]])|label1=[[Christianity in Balisca|Christianity]]|value1=42.8|color1=blue|label2=[[Irreligion in Balisca|No religion]]|value2=42.6|color2=beige|label3=[[Islam in Balisca|Islam]]|value3=4.9|color3=Green|label4=[[Hinduism in Balisca|Hinduism]]|value4=2.3|color4=darkorange|label5=[[Sikhism in Balisca|Sikhism]]|value5=2.1|color5=red|label6=[[Buddhism in Balisca|Buddhism]]|value6=1.0|color6=gold|label7=[[Judaism in Balisca|Judaism]]|value7=0.9|color7=darkblue|label8=[[w:Native American religions|Indigenous]]|value8=0.2|color8=pink|label9={{W|Paganism}}|value9=0.1|color9=lightgreen|label10=Other religions|value10=2.8|color10=magenta}}[[Basic Law: Freedom of Religious Belief|Confederal law]] prevents the establishment of a {{W|state religion}} at any level, and prohibits the government from involving itself in the private religious affairs of Baliscan citizens. Additionally, It allows the right to believe what a person, group, or {{W|religion}} wishes, but it does not allow the right to practice the religion or belief openly and outwardly in a public manner, a central facet of {{W|religious freedom}}. There has been strict [[W:Separation of church and state|separation of church and state]] since confederation, and it has co-opted the French principle of {{W|laïcité}}, in which the State does not recognize any official ''religion''. Instead, it merely recognizes certain religious organizations, according to formal legal criteria that do not address religious doctrine. In return, religious organizations are to refrain from involvement in the State's policy-making or any activity that could be interpreted as political in a court of law.
[[File:Sant Roque cathedral.jpg|left|thumb|345x345px|[[Sant Roque Cathedral|Basilica Major de Sant Roque]] in [[Alessandria]]]]
In Balisca, "freedom of religious belief" is used as a more broad replacement for both "freedom of religion" and "freedom from religion". The term "belief" is considered inclusive of all forms of {{W|irreligion}}, including {{W|atheism}}, {{W|antitheism}} and {{W|humanism}}. The practice of religion is now legally considered a private matter throughout society and the state.

[[W:Catholic Church|Roman Catholicism]] has been the predominant religion in Hesperia since colonization, though it is not nearly as actively practiced today as it was. While in the 1980s, more than 60% of the Hesperians still declared themselves to be Catholic, in the 1990s this proportion had fallen to less than 50%, in the 2000s to less than 40%, and in the 2010s it is less than 30%. According to the Baliscan Center for Sociological Research, 42.8% identified as [[W:Christianity|Christian]] (any denomination), 42.6% identify as {{W|atheists}} (27.5%) and [[W:Agnostics|agnostics]] (15.1%), 4.9% as {{W|Muslim}}, (2.7% define themselves as not practising, while 2.2% as practicing), and 9.4% as followers of other faiths (including {{W|African diaspora religions}}, {{W|Mythologies of the Indigenous peoples of the Americas|Indigenous beliefs}}, {{W|Hinduism}}, {{W|Buddhism}} etc.), as of January 2025. According to the same poll, the proportion of the irreligious is significantly higher among youth, about 89% among the 18-25-year olds. An overwhelming majority of Hesperians do not participate regularly in weekly religious worship. A July 2024 study shows that of the Hesperians who identify themselves as religious, 61% never attend services, 20.8% barely ever attend services, 15.1% attend a few times a year, 3.0% two or three times per month, 13.4% every holidays, and 0.1% multiple times per week.

During the [[Baliscan Revolution]], activists conducted a violent [[Christianity in Balisca#Anti-clericalism and anti-Catholicism|campaign of de-Christianisation]], aimed at destroying the influence of Catholic institutions on Hesperian society. Across the country clergy and churches were attacked, with [[W:Iconoclasm|iconoclasm]] driving the gutting the churches of statues, ornament, and anything considered to be of significance. By the end of the revolution, between 70-90% of all religious structures had been gutted and burned across Hesperia, while an estimated 32,000-35,000 had been killed in anti-Catholic driven violence.

== Geography ==
{{Main|Geography of Hesperia}}

Hesperia is located at the eastern extremity of the island of [[Atlantida]], making it one of the four Atlántidan communities. At its narrowest, Hesperia's mainland is separated from [[Siculia]]'s [[Republic of Punta del Sul|Punta del Sol]] by a {{convert|31|km}} stretch of the [[Augustinian Sea]], and {{convert|8.29|km}} from [[Piscaria]] by the [[Strait of tba]]. 

Consisting of a long and narrow coastal area on the east side of the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] (except for [[Trás-as-Montanyas]]), Hesperia stretches over {{convert|2082|km|abbr=on}} km north to south, and only {{convert|491|km|abbr=on}} at its widest point east to west. This encompasses a remarkable variety of climates and landscapes. It contains {{convert|406796|km2}} of land area, making it the second largest Baliscan community, with 24.90% of its total land area.  Additionally, it is the 52nd [[w:List of the largest country subdivisions by area|largest subnational division in the world]], and if it was an independent nation would be the 60th largest country in the world, larger than {{W|Zimbabwe}}. Eight-five percent of Hesperia's territory is mountainous (more than 1,000 meters (3,300 ft) above sea level); 60 percent is forested; and only about 5 percent is arable.

If independent, Hesperia would be amongst the longest north–south countries in the world. If one considers only mainland territory, Hesperia is comparable to {{W|Chile}} within this group in its narrowness from east to west, with the other long north–south countries (including [[W:Brazil|Brazil]], the [[Soviet Union]], and the [[United States]], among others) all being wider from east to west by a factor of more than 10. 
[[File:Añangabaú valley.png|left|thumb|260x260px|[[Añangabaú]] valley of the Matarrañya / Cañadapirén, located in western [[Tartessia]]]]
[[File:大霸尖山.jpg|thumb|[[Nambi-Apu'ã]] ({{Literal translation|towering ear}}) is the tallest mountain in Hesperia, at {{convert|3492|m|ft|abbr=on}} tall]]
The Hesperian relief consists of the central depression, which crosses the country longitudinally, flanked by two mountain ranges that make up about 80% of the territory: the Matarrañya to the west-form natural border with [[Bayara]], and the Coastal Range east-minor height from the Matarrañya. Hesperia's highest peak is [[Sonoro|Nambi-Apu'ã]], at {{convert|3492|m|ft|abbr=on}}, and is located in [[Basilisco]]. 

Along its dramatic coastal ranges, Hesperian has nearly {{convert|34000|mi|km|-3}} of tidal shoreline. Many seismically active {{W|fault line}}s are found, especially in the inland regions. Among the coastal mountains and the South Atlantic is a series of coastal plains, of variable length, which historically allowed for the establishing of coastal settlements and big ports. Some areas of the plains territories encompass territory on both sides of the Matarrañya / Cañadapirén.

Due to the characteristics of the territory, Hesperia is crossed by numerous rivers generally short in length and with low flow rates. They commonly extend from the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] to the {{W|Atlantic Ocean}}, flowing from West to East. In the center-north of the community, the number of rivers that form valleys of agricultural importance increases. Noteworthy are the [[tba]] with 75 km long, 142 km [[tba]], [[tba]] with 250 km and its tributary, the [[tba]] with 110 km, and [[tba]] with 240 km. Their waters mainly originate from the [[W:Snowmelt|snowmelt]] in the Matarrañyan mountains in the summer and heavy rainfall during the winter months. <gallery widths="150" perrow="3" mode="packed" heights="120" caption="The varied landscapes of Hesperia">
